IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Writ Petition (Civil) No. 1010 of 2017
CITATION: (2018) 5 SCC 1
YEAR: 2018

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: Dipak Misra, A.K. Sikri, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Ashok Bhushan

PARTIES:
Common Cause vs Union of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Article 21

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
passive euthanasia, living will, advance medical directives, right to die with dignity

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Recognized right to die with dignity as part of Article 21, validating living wills and passive euthanasia for terminally ill patients in permanent vegetative states.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Article 21.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

